    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n$$R_{\\mu\\nu} = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad ds^2 = -\\Big(1-\\tfrac{2M}{r}\\Big)dt^2 + \\Big(1-\\tfrac{2M}{r}\\Big)^{-1}dr^2 + r^2d\\Omega^2$$\n\n**Derived, in two steps.** From the ansatz $-A(r)dt^2 + B(r)dr^2 + r^2d\\Omega^2$:\n\n1. $R_{tt}/A + R_{rr}/B$ came out as $(AB)'/(rAB^2)$ — the second derivatives cancel identically. Vanishing forces $AB$ constant, and matching Minkowski at infinity gives $B = 1/A$.\n2. With that in the metric, $R_{\\theta\\theta} = 1 - A - rA' = 1 - (rA)'$, and `ode2` returned $A = (r+c)/r$. Writing $c = -2M$ finishes it.\n\nThe result was then verified Ricci-flat in all sixteen components — which also closes step 2, since only $R_{\\theta\\theta}$ was solved explicitly. $M$ is an integration constant; nothing in the field equations knows it is a mass.\n\n**One extra term.** With Killing fields $\\partial_t$ and $\\partial_\\varphi$ giving conserved $E$ and $L$ (5.03 doing work), the radial motion reduces to $\\dot r^2 = E^2 - V$ with\n\n$$V = 1 - \\frac{2M}{r} + \\frac{L^2}{r^2} - \\frac{2ML^2}{r^3},$$\n\nand $V - V_{\\text{Newton}} = -2ML^2/r^3$ exactly. **Everything Einstein predicts and Newton does not, for orbits, is that one term.** It falls off faster, which is why Mercury and not Neptune.\n\n**Two radii with no Newtonian counterpart.** Circular orbits need $L^2 = Mr^2/(r-3M)$, which diverges at $r = 3M$ — the photon sphere. Stability needs $V'' > 0$; on circular orbits $V'' \\propto (2Mr - 12M^2)$, negative below $r = 6M$ and positive above. The ISCO at $6M$ sets the inner edge of accretion discs actually observed around black holes.\n\n**The two classical tests, in numbers.**\n\n| test | computed here | observed |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mercury's perihelion | $42.98''$/century | $\\approx 43''$/century |\n| solar light deflection | $1.7512''$ | $1.98'' \\pm 0.12''$, $1.61'' \\pm 0.30''$ (1919) |\n\nThe precession came from a perturbation of the orbit equation $u'' + u = M/L^2 + 3Mu^2$: trying $u = A(1 + e\\cos\\omega\\varphi)$ makes the $e\\cos$ coefficient vanish exactly when $\\omega^2 = 1 - 6M^2/L^2$, so the radial oscillation is slower than the angular one and the ellipse does not close.\n\nThe deflection is $4M/b$, **twice** the Newtonian corpuscular value. Half comes from $g_{tt}$, which Newton has, and half from $g_{rr}$, which he has not. The 1919 eclipse measured the factor of two, not merely the existence of an effect, which is why it settled the question.\n\n**The horizon is not there.** The Kretschmann scalar came out $48M^2/r^6$ — finite at $r = 2M$ (indeed $3/4M^4$, unremarkable) and divergent only at $r = 0$. A curvature scalar is coordinate-independent, so that settles it. Eddington–Finkelstein coordinates then produce\n\n$$ds^2 = -\\Big(1-\\tfrac{2M}{r}\\Big)dv^2 + 2\\,dv\\,dr + r^2d\\Omega^2,$$\n\nwhose $(v,r)$ block at $r = 2M$ is $\\begin{psmallmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{psmallmatrix}$ with determinant $-1$: non-degenerate, every entry finite. This is 4.04's polar-coordinate plane again — $\\Gamma \\ne 0$ with $R \\equiv 0$ — at astronomical scale.\n\nWhat *is* true at $r = 2M$ is that $g_{vv} = 0$, so $v = \\text{const}$ is null and the light cones have tipped past vertical. A horizon is a statement about **causal structure** (6.01), not about curvature diverging.\n\n**Finite one way, infinite the other.** Falling from rest at infinity, the proper time from $r = 10M$ to $r = 2M$ is $13.57M$ — finite. The coordinate time to reach $2M + \\delta$ grew as $25.2, 29.9, 34.6, 39.2$ for $\\delta = 10^{-1}\\dots10^{-4}$: increments of $4.61$ per decade, which is $2M\\ln 10 = 4.605$. The infaller crosses; the distant observer never sees it. Only proper time is what a clock reads.\n\n## Next\n\n**6.03 — Symplectic Geometry and Mechanics** changes the structure on the manifold from a symmetric form to an antisymmetric one. Darboux's theorem says there are no local invariants at all — no symplectic curvature — so the whole subject is global from the start. It is the geometry underneath Hamiltonian mechanics, and the backbone of [control-systems-journey](https://github.com/cmsd2/control-systems-journey).\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Callahan, J.J. (2000) *The Geometry of Spacetime*. New York: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-6736-0](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4757-6736-0). Chapters 6–8. The derivation and the tests at this level, with the geometry kept in front throughout.\n- ★ Landau, L.D. and Lifshitz, E.M. (1975) *The Classical Theory of Fields*. 4th revised English edn. Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2. Oxford: Pergamon. §§95–101: the field equations, the Schwarzschild solution, and both classical tests, in about twenty pages.\n- Misner, C.W., Thorne, K.S. and Wheeler, J.A. (1973) *Gravitation*. San Francisco: Freeman. Chapters 23, 25 and 31–32. Exhaustive on the horizon and on what Eddington–Finkelstein and Kruskal coordinates each reveal.\n- Wald, R.M. (1984) *General Relativity*. Chicago: University of Chicago Press. Chapter 6 for Schwarzschild done carefully, including a proper statement and proof of Birkhoff's theorem, which this notebook assumes.\n- Dyson, F.W., Eddington, A.S. and Davidson, C. (1920) 'A determination of the deflection of light by the Sun's gravitational field, from observations made at the total eclipse of May 29, 1919', *Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A*, 220, pp. 291–333. The measurement itself.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell was executed and the numerical agreements above are computed output, not quoted. Birkhoff's theorem is assumed, not proved: the ansatz here already restricts $A$ and $B$ to functions of $r$ alone. One Maxima note: the tortoise coordinate cannot be written `r*` inside a block comment, because the asterisk-slash sequence terminates the comment.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}